In October 1961, the ground started to crack and rumble close to the village. A new [[@volcano|volcano]] vent opened just behind the houses. The islanders were taken off by boat and eventually brought to England.

They lived near Southampton for nearly two years, in a very different, crowded world of cars and shops. Many were unhappy. In 1963, when scientists said it was safe, most of them voted to go home. They found their houses mostly unharmed, though [[lava|lava]] had buried their old fish factory.

{{story:volcano}}

स्याही तो बचाव का बस आधा हिस्सा है। ऑक्टोपस, स्क्विड और कटलफ़िश जानवरों के एक समूह में आते हैं, जिसे [[cephalopod|सेफ़ेलोपॉड]] कहते हैं, यानी "सिर-पैर"। ये अपने शरीर में पानी खींचकर साइफ़न नाम की एक नली से ज़ोर से बाहर फेंक सकते हैं। यह धार उन्हें तेज़ी से पीछे की ओर धकेल देती है। पर धार चलाना थकाने वाला है। ऑक्टोपस के तीन दिल होते हैं, और धार चलाते समय मुख्य दिल रुक जाता है। इसलिए ऑक्टोपस आम तौर पर रेंगकर चलते हैं, और धार को मुसीबत के वक़्त के लिए बचाकर रखते हैं।

उसी पल कई ऑक्टोपस रंग भी बदल लेते हैं। उनकी त्वचा की ख़ास [[cell|कोशिकाएँ]] एक सेकंड से भी कम में उन्हें फीका, गहरा या धारीदार-चितकबरा बना सकती हैं। स्याही के साथ मिलकर यह [[@camouflage|कैमूफ़्लाज]] शिकारी के लिए यह समझना बहुत मुश्किल कर देता है कि अभी-अभी हुआ क्या।

{{story:ink}}

Natural rubber comes from the milky sap, called latex, of the rubber tree, which grows wild in the Amazon rainforest of [[@brazil-amazon|Brazil]]. People in Central and South America had used it for thousands of years to make balls and waterproof cloth. Europeans began bringing samples home in the 1700s.

In 1770, the English scientist Joseph Priestley, better known for discovering oxygen, noted that a small cube of this gum was excellent for rubbing out pencil marks. He added that a London instrument maker, Edward Nairne, sold cubes about a centimetre across for three shillings each, a high price at the time. Because it "rubbed", people started calling it rubber. The name spread to the material itself. In Britain and India, people still say "rubber" for an eraser, while Americans say "eraser".

{{story:inventions-accidents}}

The most common cold viruses are rhinoviruses. There are more than 160 kinds of rhinovirus alone, plus many other cold [[virus|viruses]]. When your body beats one, it remembers that exact virus, but the next cold is often a different one. That's why there is no single cold [[@vaccines|vaccine]].

Young children catch more colds than adults, sometimes six or more a year, because they haven't met as many viruses yet. Each cold teaches their [[@immune-system|immune system]] a little more.

{{story:soap-germs}}

Most glues dry when water or another liquid evaporates out of them. Superglue does the opposite: water makes it set. Its small [[molecule|molecules]] are waiting to link up into long chains called [[polymer|polymers]]. The slightest touch of water starts a chain reaction, and the chains lock into a hard plastic web.

There's a faint film of moisture on almost every surface, and plenty on human skin. That's why superglue grips your fingers in a few seconds, and why a tube left open hardens inside.

{{story:glue-sticky}}

The word appeared in 1999. That year, a group of programmers in Canada met to work on free software called OpenBSD, and the company Sun Microsystems ran a similar coding challenge in the United States. Both used the word.

Programmers had been doing late-night building sessions for years. But hackathons really spread in the 2000s and 2010s, as companies, universities and even governments started running them. Several big tech companies hold in-house hackathons, and some features of well-known apps began as weekend experiments.

{{story:startups-entrepreneurs}}

Jallianwala Bagh was surrounded by walls and the backs of houses, with only a few narrow ways in and out. Dyer placed his soldiers at the main entrance, which also blocked the main exit.

He gave no order to disperse. The soldiers fired into the crowd, aiming where people were most crowded. There was nowhere to hide. Some people tried to climb the walls, and some jumped into a well in the garden. A curfew that night meant the wounded could not be helped. Bullet marks can still be seen in the walls today.

{{story:tagore}}

For years, the most popular explanation was a deadly storm. In 2004, scientists studying some skulls found short, deep cracks on the top of the heads. Something round and hard had hit them from above.

Local songs tell of the goddess Nanda Devi sending iron-hard hail to punish travellers who angered her. Some researchers suggested a real [[@hailstorms-india|hailstorm]] with stones as big as cricket balls struck a group in the open. With nowhere to shelter at that height, many could have died within minutes. It remains a guess, and it may explain only some deaths.

{{story:dna}}

In the mountain town of Lalibela, eleven churches were carved straight down into solid volcanic rock, probably in the 12th and 13th centuries, under King Lalibela. Builders dug deep trenches to free a block of stone, then hollowed out rooms, windows, columns and roofs from inside it.

The best known, the Church of St George, is shaped like a cross when seen from above. It is a little like the [[@ellora-kailasa|Kailasa temple at Ellora]] in Maharashtra, which was also carved from the top down out of a single hill. Pilgrims in white robes still pray there today. Ethiopia was one of the earliest countries to adopt Christianity, in the 4th century, and about two-fifths of Ethiopians belong to the Ethiopian Orthodox Church. About a third are Muslim.

{{story:human-evolution}}

Sunlight is a mix of every colour. When it enters water, red light is soaked up first, then yellow and green. Blue goes deepest and is scattered back out. That is why deep water looks blue, a cousin of the reason the [[@sky-blue|sky is blue]].

Near the shore, the water is shallow. Light bounces off the pale bed and fine clay before the red has all been absorbed, so you see green and turquoise. Wind adds another layer. Ripples reflect the sky, and a cloudy sky makes the surface look silver or grey. As the sun moves, the angle of light changes, and so does the whole lake.

{{story:sky-blue}}

Before 1840, the person receiving a letter usually paid for it. Then Britain introduced the Penny Black, the first sticky postage stamp. The sender paid in advance, and posting a letter became cheap and simple.

Today a letter goes into a postbox, gets collected and sorted, and travels by van, train or plane. In India the six-digit PIN code, introduced in 1972, helps sorters send it to the right [[@post-office|post office]]. Each digit has a job. The first stands for one of India's postal regions, the next two narrow it to a smaller zone and a sorting district, and the last three point to the delivery post office. The United States started its five-digit ZIP codes in 1963. India Post runs well over 1.5 lakh post offices, from big city buildings to tiny rooms in Himalayan villages.

{{story:post-office}}
